    {
      "title": "Direct and indirect (reported) speech",
      "html": "<p><b>Direct speech</b> gives the exact words of a speaker inside quotation marks: <i>She said, \"I am tired.\"</i></p>\n  <p><b>Indirect (reported) speech</b> gives the meaning without quotation marks and usually shifts the tense one step back.</p>\n  <h4>Tense shifts</h4>\n  <table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse\"><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;padding:4px 8px\">Direct</th><th style=\"padding:4px 8px\">Indirect</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>simple present</td><td>simple past</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>present continuous</td><td>past continuous</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>present perfect / simple past</td><td>past perfect</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>will</td><td>would</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>can</td><td>could</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>may</td><td>might</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>must</td><td>had to</td></tr></table>\n  <h4>Other changes</h4>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Time and place words shift: <i>today\u2192that day, tomorrow\u2192the next day, yesterday\u2192the day before, now\u2192then, here\u2192there, this\u2192that.</i></li>\n    <li>Pronouns adjust to the reporter's perspective.</li>\n    <li>Questions become statements: use <i>if / whether</i> for yes/no questions; keep WH-words for WH-questions; drop <i>do / does / did</i>.</li>\n    <li>Commands use <i>told + object + to-infinitive</i>: <i>He said, \"Sit down.\" \u2192 He told them to sit down.</i></li>\n  </ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Report: <i>She said, \"I will help you tomorrow.\"</i>",
          "a": "<i>She said (that) she would help me the next day.</i>"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Complex concord and grammatical exceptions",
      "html": "<p>Some concord (agreement) situations trip up even careful writers.</p>\n  <ol>\n    <li><i>Neither of / either of / each of / one of + plural noun</i> \u2192 singular verb: <i>Neither of the boys <b>is</b> here.</i></li>\n    <li><i>None of + plural noun</i> \u2014 plural verb: <i>None of the students <b>have</b> arrived.</i></li>\n    <li><i>The rich / the poor / the sick</i> \u2014 plural: <i>The rich <b>are</b> not always happy.</i></li>\n    <li><i>A pair of, a series of, a variety of, a bunch of</i> \u2014 singular: <i>A series of tests <b>has</b> been scheduled.</i></li>\n    <li><i>More than one</i> \u2014 singular: <i>More than one student <b>was</b> late.</i></li>\n    <li>Countries and titles ending in -s (<i>the United States, the Philippines, Wuthering Heights</i>) \u2014 singular.</li>\n    <li>Percentages / fractions agree with the noun after <i>of</i>: <i>Two-thirds of the cake <b>is</b> gone; two-thirds of the students <b>are</b> ready.</i></li>\n  </ol>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Correct: <i>None of the water (is/are) contaminated.</i>",
          "a": "<b>is</b> \u2014 <i>water</i> is uncountable/singular."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Question tags, relatives and syntactic analysis",
      "html": "<p><b>Question tags</b> confirm or seek agreement.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Positive statement \u2192 negative tag: <i>She sings well, doesn't she?</i></li>\n    <li>Negative statement \u2192 positive tag: <i>He has not left, has he?</i></li>\n    <li>Special: <i>I am, aren't I?; Let's go, shall we?; Open the door, will you?; Everyone is here, aren't they?; Nobody came, did they?</i></li>\n  </ul>\n  <p><b>Relative pronouns</b>: <i>who</i> (people), <i>which</i> (things), <i>whose</i> (possession), <i>that</i> (defining), <i>whom</i> (object). Non-defining clauses take commas: <i>My father, who lives in Kano, is a lawyer.</i></p>\n  <h4>Syntactic analysis</h4>\n  <p>Break a sentence into <b>subject + predicate</b>, then the predicate into <b>verb + object / complement + adjuncts</b>. Identify each clause: main (independent) or subordinate.</p>\n  <p><i>Although it was raining (subordinate: adverbial), the boys, who had planned the game (relative), continued to play in the field (main).</i></p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Add a tag: <i>She has finished the assignment, __?</i>",
          "a": "<i>hasn't she?</i>"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Relative pronouns and clauses",
      "html": "<p><b>Relative pronouns and clauses</b> is a key part of Grammar and Structure (SSS 3, English). Master the meaning, the main points and how it is tested in JAMB and WAEC questions on this topic.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Pronouns adjust to the reporter's perspective.</li><li>Relative pronouns : who (people), which (things), whose (possession), that (defining), whom (object).</li><li>Non-defining clauses take commas: My father, who lives in Kano, is a lawyer.</li><li>Although it was raining (subordinate: adverbial), the boys, who had planned the game (relative), continued to play in the field (main).</li><li>Commas mark non-defining clauses.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Relative pronoun for people (subject):<br/><b>A:</b> who \u2014 'Who' \u2014 subject for people.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Relative pronoun for possession:<br/><b>A:</b> whose \u2014 'Whose' shows possession.</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Relative pronoun for people (subject):",
          "a": "who \u2014 'Who' \u2014 subject for people."
        },
        {
          "q": "Relative pronoun for possession:",
          "a": "whose \u2014 'Whose' shows possession."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Revision and exam focus",
      "html": "<p>Revise the whole topic by working through its key points in order, then test yourself with the 50-question quiz for this topic. Pay attention to definitions, differences between similar terms, and questions that ask you to apply the topic rather than only recall it.</p><h4>Worked examples</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Report: <i>She said, \"I am tired.\"</i><br/><b>A:</b> She said she was tired. \u2014 Present \u2192 past.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Report: <i>He said, \"I have finished the work.\"</i><br/><b>A:</b> He said he had finished the work. \u2014 Present perfect \u2192 past perfect.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Report: <i>They said, \"We will come tomorrow.\"</i><br/><b>A:</b> They said they would come the next day. \u2014 will\u2192would; tomorrow\u2192the next day.</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Report: <i>She said, \"I am tired.\"</i>",
          "a": "She said she was tired."
        },
        {
          "q": "Report: <i>He said, \"I have finished the work.\"</i>",
          "a": "He said he had finished the work."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
